WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.950
NARRATOR: Hawaii is the
only place in the US

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.136
where humpbacks breed
and nurse their young.

00:00:10.136 --> 00:00:14.240
[WHALE CALLS]

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.520
Born with very
little fat, calves

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.610
would soon freeze to death
in the cold waters of Alaska.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.430
Mothers come to these
clear shallow waters

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.102
to avoid predators.

00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:36.574
[SPLASHING WAVES]

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.180
But there's a catch.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.250
There's no food here.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:56.680
The moms go hungry, losing
up to 40% of their weight

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:57.820
raising their calves.

00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:05.500
[WHALE CALLS]

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.990
It's a sacrifice that pays off.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.620
Humpback populations
are increasing.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:28.750
The park is just one place to
see these giants of the deep,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.250
but they are not its
biggest attraction.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.350
Two massive volcanoes are.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:39.730
The first Kilauea.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.220
One of the most active
volcanoes on earth.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:02:00.390
The lava lake inside this crater
can be more than 750 feet deep.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.975
Its lava flows can reach
the sea 10 miles away.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.495
Kilauea is so big, that even
its vents and craters are huge.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:23.170
Starting in 1983, one of these
vents erupted for 35 years.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:28.230
It produced so much lava that it
covered the coast road, burying

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.420
it more than 100 feet deep.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.670
To the west is the
second volcano,

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.160
and it's the biggest
on the planet.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.740
This is Mauna Loa, rising more
than 56,000 feet above its

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.170
base, much taller than even Mt.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.070
Everest.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.100
The park exists
thanks to eruptions

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.395
over hundreds of
thousands of years.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:12.910
Where hardened lava forms a
solid foundation for life.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:24.080
Close to the source, poisonous
gas fumes escape from hot vents

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.180
called fumaroles.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:32.455
This landscape is often
swept clean by molten lava.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.650
It's one of the most barren and
toxic environments on Earth.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:48.290
Nature has had to adapt to
living in this land of fire.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.640
A wingless larva cricket.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.180
After an eruption, she
is the first creature

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:23.190
to take up residence in
this hostile landscape.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.620
The ultimate pioneer.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.810
No larger than a
fingernail, she survives

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.450
by eating morsels
of food that billow

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:40.815
in with the ocean breeze.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.540
Nobody knows where she
comes from, where she goes,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.760
or how long she lives.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.600
In many ways, she's a mystery.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

